| ID | Test | Mechanism |
|---|---|---|
| S4-F10 | 2 tests | `send("error")` precedes `_rollback`; an ENOSPC append or throwing `emit` skips it. Half venv survives. |
| S4-F11 | 1 test | `downloaded` is whole-repo/size-only, so a user's 80% partial reads "missing", gets resumed, then `evict` deletes the merged ledger entry — including their bytes. |
| S4-F12 | 1 test | Sweep's `except` logs but never appends `not_evicted`, unlike the inner path 6 lines up. |
| S4-F13 | 1 test | Empty lock ⇒ `pid = None` ⇒ liveness check skipped ⇒ live owner's lock removed. Distinct from the accepted dead-owner race. |
| S4-F14 | 1 test + grep | No `fsync` anywhere in fetch.py; no downstream byte re-check, so a right-size/wrong-bytes blob reaches `stage_a`. |

Every finding was red-checked: applied a minimal fix, watched the test go green (existing nearby suites still passing), reverted, watched it fail again. One negative result worth flagging: for F14, adding a verify loop directly inside `quickbench.plan` breaks 48 existing tests, because the suite's hand-built rows carry fake hashes — so the fix needs an injected seam, not a hard call. That's recorded in the F14 report rather than implemented.

**Not verified:** no network, model, engine, server, subprocess or app was run. F14's fsync half is source evidence only (a power loss can't be staged in pytest), and F13's race is staged by state rather than by two real processes.

